---
source: crates/daemon/tests/cli_help.rs
expression: stdout
---
Ask for writing help and read the proposal. Nothing in the draft changes until you accept it with `mxr compose apply`.

With nothing typed the model writes the message; with text it finishes it, keeping your sentences and adding what the message still needs (greeting, sign-off, an answer to each question you haven't covered); with a selection it rewrites only that part. Anything only you can decide comes back as a `[[?: ...]]` gap, and a draft with a gap can't be sent. Calls your LLM once; takes as long as the model does.

Usage: mxr compose assist [OPTIONS] <--draft <DRAFT_ID>|--reply-to <MESSAGE_ID>|--forward <MESSAGE_ID>|--new>

Options:
      --draft <DRAFT_ID>
          Help with a stored draft. Its text is used unless `--text` is given

      --reply-to <MESSAGE_ID>
          Help with a reply to this message

      --all
          With `--reply-to`: the reply goes to everyone

      --forward <MESSAGE_ID>
          Help with a note forwarding this message; needs `--to`

      --new
          Help with a new message; needs `--account`, `--to` and `--subject`

      --account <ACCOUNT>
          With `--new`: the account to write from (name, email or id)

      --to <TO>
          With `--forward` or `--new`: who it goes to (comma-separated or repeated)

      --subject <SUBJECT>
          With `--new`: the subject line

      --text <PATH>
          The text you have typed so far: a file path, or `-` for stdin. Only your own text, with no signature or forward header. Empty or absent means nothing typed (except with `--draft`, which reads the stored draft)

      --select <START..END>
          Work on part of the text only, as byte offsets `START..END` that fall between whole characters

      --write
          Write the whole message, ignoring any text

      --finish
          Keep what is typed and add what the message still needs

      --polish
          Fix and tighten what is typed without adding to it

      --instruction <TEXT>
          What you want said or changed, in your words

      --model <MODEL_ID>
          Use one configured model for this draft only

      --revise <PROPOSAL_ID>
          Revise an earlier proposal: the instruction is applied to it. Your own text stays what `--text` (or the stored draft) says

      --shorter
          Make it shorter

      --warmer
          Make it warmer

      --formal
          Make it more formal

      --proofread
          Fix typos, spelling and grammar only

      --format <FORMAT>
          [possible values: table, json, jsonl, csv, ids]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
